---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Colophon"
  description="Notes on the design and construction of Brian Lerner's website."
  image="/og/colophon.png"
>
  <header class="page-header">
    <p class="eyebrow">Colophon</p>
    <h1>Why this site looks like a readout</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="prose colophon-copy">
    <p>
      I spend a lot of time looking at flowsheets, scorecards, and model outputs. They are
      versions of the same invention: a way to decide which observations deserve attention
      and how they should sit beside one another. The cool greys, small labels, and ruled
      lines here borrow that visual language without pretending this website is a clinical
      tool.
    </p>
    <p>
      Much of my work is the practice of deciding what data counts. That includes what is
      collected, what is missing, and what should be excluded. The publications filter keeps
      its excluded work visible for the same reason: missingness can be informative.
    </p>
    <p class="build-note">
      Built with Astro, TypeScript, and plain CSS. The
      <a href={site.repository}>source is public</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .colophon-copy {
    margin-top: 36px;
  }

  .build-note {
    margin-top: 40px;
    padding-top: 16px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font: 400 0.75rem/1.6 "IBM Plex Mono", monospace;
  }
</style>
